Beryllium: the browser cuts the rights of every descriptor it sends a confined renderer

Purpose: Phase 2 increment 5 (plan/14-RENDERER-SANDBOX.md; feature matrix
  S-IPC-01; plan/06 section 3.2; adversarial review plan/16 BER-05). Until
  now a confined renderer limited what it received (the receive paths in
  beryllium-descriptor-rights.patch); a compromised renderer need not run
  that code, so the receiver's limit is defence in depth, not a boundary.
  The boundary is the sender: a descriptor's rights travel with it through
  sendmsg(), so what the browser cuts before sending, the renderer can
  never have.
  - content/browser/child_process_launcher_helper.cc: when a renderer is
    launched with the lockdown feature on, its pid is registered as a
    confined peer (base::freebsd::ExpectConfinedPeer) before the Mojo
    invitation is sent, so the first message carrying a handle is covered,
    and forgotten when the launcher helper goes, since the kernel may reuse
    the pid. The sandbox type comes from the launcher's delegate; the
    feature from base/posix/capsicum_rights.h.
  - mojo/core/ipcz_driver/transport.cc, Transport::Transmit(): the one
    place a Mojo transport sends platform handles. On a transport whose
    remote process is a confined peer, every handle is cut to its kind's
    rights (base::freebsd::LimitSentDescriptor): read-only files, writable
    files (the new kWritableFile kind, for files handed over on purpose),
    sockets, kqueues, eventfds and pipes, shared memory; a kind the policy
    does not have (a device, a directory, a terminal) loses every right and
    is logged. The remote process is known on the transports the browser
    made for the children it launched; transports between two children (a
    renderer and the GPU process, introduced by the browser) carry no
    remote process here, so what the GPU process sends a renderer is still
    covered only by the receiver's limit.
  What this does not cover yet: descriptors the browser passes at launch
    (they are in the table the lockdown sweeps), and the sandbox IPC
    channel's replies (shared memory the browser creates, already limited
    at creation by beryllium-anonymous-shared-memory.patch).
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on beryllium-descriptor-rights.patch
  (the feature, the peer registry, LimitSentDescriptor, kWritableFile).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled (build 0.20.3.13).
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py test_lockdown_log: the
  suite runs the sandbox class with --vmodule=capsicum_rights=1, counts the
  browser's "cut ... bound for confined renderer" lines per renderer and,
  under the gate on a build that reports the lockdown's thread count,
  requires at least one; test_confined_renderer_descriptors_follow_the_policy
  keeps checking the result from outside with procstat. A verification
  that the sender alone suffices (the receiver's limit switched off) needs
  a development-only build switch and is listed as open in plan/14.
security-review: required (IPC boundary, descriptor rights)

